# Declare Agent Topology

This tutorial declares an order desk with fixed named members and a nested team, and then starts it under one owner. Run the cells in order.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "migration/consolidated-v3"}
])
```

## Declare members

A `topology` section lists members with `agent`. The second argument is an Agent module or a definition. `count` defaults to 1 and `initial_state` defaults to `%{}`.

Both `count` and `initial_state` are static definition data.

```elixir
defmodule TopologyGuide.Packer do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "order_packer"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{
             station: Jido.Joi.string() |> Jido.Joi.default("unassigned"),
             packed: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)
           })
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/orders/packer"

    route "order.pack", as: :pack do
      action %{items: items},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{items: Jido.Joi.integer()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | packed: context.agent_state.packed + items}}
      end
    end
  end
end

defmodule TopologyGuide.Desk do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "order_desk"

  topology do
    agent :auditor, TopologyGuide.Packer do
      initial_state %{station: "audit"}
    end

    agent :packers, TopologyGuide.Packer do
      count 2
      initial_state %{station: "north"}
    end
  end
end

TopologyGuide.Desk.definition().topology.agents
|> Enum.map(&{&1.name, &1.count, &1.initial_state})
```

The cell returns plain static data:

```text
[
  {:auditor, 1, %{station: "audit"}},
  {:packers, 2, %{station: "north"}}
]
```

## Bind members without starting them

`Jido.Agent.Topology.bind/3` resolves each member definition and returns its
static count and initial state. It starts nothing.

```elixir
desk = TopologyGuide.Desk.instantiate!(id: "desk-preview")
resolver = fn definition, _opts -> {:ok, definition} end

{:ok, bound} = Jido.Agent.Topology.bind(desk, desk.state, resolver)
Enum.map(bound, &{&1.name, &1.count, &1.state})
```

The cell returns `[{:auditor, 1, %{station: "audit"}}, {:packers, 2, %{station: "north"}}]`.

## Start the owner

`Jido.start_agent/3` starts the owner. Members start under the owner's activation, so wait for `Jido.AgentServer.await_ready/2` before you use them.

```elixir
{:ok, _instance} = Jido.start(name: TopologyGuide.Jido)
{:ok, owner} = Jido.start_agent(TopologyGuide.Jido, TopologyGuide.Desk, id: "desk-1")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(owner)

Jido.agent_count(TopologyGuide.Jido)
```

The cell returns `4`: the owner, the auditor, and two packers. An Agent with members needs a supervised activation, so `Jido.AgentServer.start_link/1` rejects it.

## Read and address members

`Jido.AgentServer.members/3` returns live PIDs in slot order. Each member is an ordinary AgentServer with its own state.

```elixir
[auditor] = Jido.AgentServer.members(owner, :auditor)
[first_packer, second_packer] = packers = Jido.AgentServer.members(owner, :packers)

{:ok, pack} = TopologyGuide.Packer.pack_signal(%{items: 4})
{:ok, _committed, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(first_packer, pack)

Enum.map([auditor | packers], &Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(&1).agent.state)
```

Only the first packer changed:

```text
[
  %{station: "audit", packed: 0},
  %{station: "north", packed: 4},
  %{station: "north", packed: 0}
]
```

`Jido.AgentServer.children/2` returns all children, with each worker and activation PID. Its keys for members are generated, so do not match on them. Use `members/3` instead.

## Tell slots apart

Slots of one member start with the same `initial_state`. An `initialize/2` function on the member module receives the member name and slot, and can make each copy different. Outside a topology these context values are `nil`.

```elixir
defmodule TopologyGuide.Lane do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "order_lane"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{lane: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)})
  end

  def initialize(state, %{slot: nil}), do: {:ok, state}
  def initialize(state, %{slot: slot}), do: {:ok, Map.put(state, :lane, slot)}
end

defmodule TopologyGuide.Conveyor do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "order_conveyor"

  topology do
    agent :lanes, TopologyGuide.Lane, count: 2
  end
end

{:ok, conveyor} = Jido.start_agent(TopologyGuide.Jido, TopologyGuide.Conveyor, id: "conveyor-1")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(conveyor)

Jido.AgentServer.members(conveyor, :lanes) |> Enum.map(&Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(&1).agent.state.lane)
```

The cell returns `[1, 2]`.

## Nest a team

A member can declare its own topology. Each level owns the level below it, so `members/3` reads one level at a time.

```elixir
defmodule TopologyGuide.Team do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "order_team"

  topology do
    agent :packers, TopologyGuide.Packer, count: 2
  end
end

defmodule TopologyGuide.Warehouse do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "order_warehouse"

  topology do
    agent :team, TopologyGuide.Team
  end
end

{:ok, warehouse} = Jido.start_agent(TopologyGuide.Jido, TopologyGuide.Warehouse, id: "warehouse-1")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(warehouse)

[team] = Jido.AgentServer.members(warehouse, :team)
length(Jido.AgentServer.members(team, :packers))
```

The cell returns `2`.

Do not declare a cycle. A module that lists itself as a member, or two modules that list each other, fail to compile. The `CompileError` names the file, the line, and `:recursive_membership`.

## Stop the owner

Stopping an owner stops its whole subtree. Stop each owner from this tutorial and count what remains.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(TopologyGuide.Jido, owner)
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(TopologyGuide.Jido, conveyor)
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(TopologyGuide.Jido, warehouse)

Enum.find(1..100, fn _attempt ->
  Process.sleep(10)
  Jido.agent_count(TopologyGuide.Jido) == 0
end) != nil
```

The cell returns `true`.

## See validation errors

A module compiles without checking its members, because they may not be compiled yet. Full checks run on the first call to `definition/0` or when you start the Agent. `Jido.Agent.define/1` runs them at once on Map input and returns the error.

```elixir
member = fn fields ->
  Jido.Agent.define(%{
    name: "invalid_desk",
    topology: %{agents: [Map.merge(%{name: :packer, definition: TopologyGuide.Packer}, fields)]}
  })
end

{:error, %{code: :reserved_name}} = member.(%{name: :root})
{:error, %{code: :invalid_positive}} = member.(%{count: 0})
{:error, %{code: :invalid_state}} = member.(%{initial_state: %{packed: "many"}})
{:error, %{code: :unresolved_definition}} = member.(%{definition: TopologyGuide.Missing})
:rejected
```

The cell returns `:rejected` after every case fails with its expected code. A member's `initial_state` may hold only fields from that member's schema.

